<section class="phero"><div class="phero-media"><img src="/assets/img/photos/nashua-city-hall.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/nashua-city-hall-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/nashua-city-hall.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="800" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><a href="/new-hampshire-cities/">Cities</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">Nashua</span></nav><p class="kicker">Merrimack Valley · Hillsborough County</p><h1>Nashua, NH Insurance Appraiser</h1><p class="lede">Nashua sits right on the state line to the south, but its claims are settled under New Hampshire policies and New Hampshire practice. We help Nashua owners push back on low estimates through the appraisal clause.</p><div class="btns"><a class="btn btn-gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-line-light" href="/contact-nh-appraiser/">Free claim review</a></div><ul class="phero-chips"><li><small>County</small><b>Hillsborough County</b></li><li><small>Region</small><b>Merrimack Valley</b></li><li><small>Housing</small><b>mill-era multifamily downtown, tree-lined older homes and newer subdivisions</b></li></ul></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<div class="local-card"><div><h2 class="local-t">At a glance</h2><p><b>Areas we cover:</b> French Hill, Crown Hill, the Tree Streets, South Nashua, the Millyard, North End.</p><p><b>Storm history:</b> The October 2011 “Snowtober” nor’easter dropped heavy, wet snow on trees still in full leaf across southern Hillsborough County, and Nashua yards and roofs took the brunt of the falling limbs.</p></div><ul class="chips"><li>Hail</li><li>Microbursts</li><li>Frozen pipes</li><li>Ice dams</li><li>Tree strikes</li></ul></div>

<h2>Two rivers, many kinds of house</h2>
<p>The Nashua River cuts through the middle of the city before meeting the Merrimack on its eastern edge, and the neighborhoods arrange themselves around both. French Hill and the blocks near the old Millyard hold closely packed multifamily buildings with porches stacked on porches. The Tree Streets are older single-family homes under big canopies. Crown Hill mixes modest early-20th-century houses with later infill. South Nashua is largely post-1970 subdivisions, townhouse complexes and condos near the retail corridor.</p>
<p>No single estimating template fits all of that, which is often where a disagreement starts.</p>
<h2>Hail on the southern tier</h2>
<p>The southern tier of the state picks up most of New Hampshire’s summer hail, and Nashua is squarely in it. Hail claims in South Nashua subdivisions tend to turn on two questions: whether the roof is functionally damaged or only “cosmetically” marked, and whether a partial repair can match what is left. Many policies leave room for argument on both. If your adjuster approved a few squares on one slope and you think the whole roof is gone, the gap is usually about scope – an amount-of-loss issue appraisal can resolve. Our <a href="/nh-appraisal-services/roof-and-hail-appraisal/">roof and hail appraisal</a> page covers what we look at.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Matching</b>When replacement shingles or siding no longer match what is on the house, the policy language and the facts both matter. Keep a sample of the old material and photos of every elevation.</div>
<h2>The trees that shade the Tree Streets</h2>
<p>Mature oaks and maples are the reason people want to live on these streets, and also the reason for a lot of claims. When a limb comes through a roof, the estimate has to cover crane work, tree removal under the policy’s debris provisions, framing, roofing and the interior below. Insurers sometimes split those pieces up and cap them separately. We put them back together and price the whole job. See <a href="/nh-appraisal-services/falling-tree-claim-appraisal/">falling-tree claim appraisal</a>.</p>
<h2>Condos, townhouses and the master policy</h2>
<p>A big share of Nashua housing is owned through associations. Water that starts in one unit and ends up in three, or ice-dam leaks through a shared roof, raise questions about which policy pays what. Read the association’s declarations and your own unit policy together; the answer is usually in the overlap. When the association and its carrier can’t agree on the cost of repairs, appraisal may be available to the association too.</p>
<p>One more Nashua pattern: the closely built multifamily blocks on French Hill and near the Millyard share walls, roofs and sometimes heating systems. A fire or water loss in one building can spill into the next, and two owners with two carriers may end up arguing over the same party wall. Getting both scopes written to the same standard keeps one side from being shortchanged.</p>
<h2>Working with us from Nashua</h2>
<p>New Hampshire Insurance Appraiser reviews estimates, serves as a policyholder’s appointed appraiser and acts as a neutral umpire when both sides ask. We are not lawyers or public adjusters; coverage and legal deadlines are questions for a New Hampshire attorney. To compare your figures against ours, try the <a href="/nh-claim-calculators/gap-and-fee-estimator/">gap and fee estimator</a> or <a href="/contact-nh-appraiser/">contact us</a>.</p>

<h2>Appraisal help available in Nashua</h2>
<p>Every New Hampshire Insurance Appraiser service is available in Nashua and the surrounding towns, including:</p>
<ul><li><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/appraisal-clause-representation-nh/">Policyholder’s Appraiser on the Panel</a></li><li><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/homeowners-claim-appraisal-nh/">Single-Family Home Claims</a></li><li><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/nh-commercial-hoa-appraisal/">Condo, Association &amp; Business Claims</a></li><li><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/roof-and-hail-appraisal/">Roof Damage &amp; Hail Disputes</a></li><li><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/wind-microburst-tornado-appraisal/">Microburst &amp; Windstorm Losses</a></li><li><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/tropical-storm-hurricane-appraisal-nh/">Tropical Remnants &amp; Coastal Storms</a></li></ul>
<h2>Asked by Nashua owners</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>I commute out of state. Does that change anything about my Nashua claim?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>No. Your home’s policy, the property’s location in New Hampshire and the policy’s own appraisal clause are what govern the process.</p></div></details><details><summary>The adjuster says my hail damage is only cosmetic. Can I still demand appraisal?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>It depends on your policy. Some forms exclude cosmetic damage outright, which is a coverage question. If the dispute is about how much of the roof needs replacing, that is usually an amount-of-loss question suited to appraisal.</p></div></details></div>
